/// Website-parity feature: English intros (tafsir-style commentary) for
/// all 114 surahs. Each entry covers the meaning of the name, the
/// revelation type and verse count, and one or two key themes.
const Map<int, String> surahIntros = {
  1: "The opening chapter of the Quran, recited in every unit of prayer. Though revealed in Makkah with only seven verses, it holds the essence of the whole Quran: praise of Allah, His mercy and mastery of the Day of Judgment, the pledge to worship Him alone, and the plea for guidance to the straight path.",
  2: "The longest chapter of the Quran, revealed in Madinah. Named after the story of the cow the Israelites were commanded to sacrifice, it lays the foundations of the Muslim community: faith, prayer, fasting, charity, Hajj, family law, and finance — alongside the stories of Adam, Ibrahim, and Bani Isra'il.",
  3: "Revealed in Madinah, this chapter centers on the family of Imran — Maryam, her son Isa, Zakariya and Yahya. It draws lessons from the Battle of Uhud, warns against division after victory, and calls the People of the Book to the common word of pure monotheism.",
  4: "A Madinan chapter devoted largely to the rights of women, orphans, and the vulnerable: marriage, dowry, and inheritance laws. It also sets rules for family life, justice in testimony, and relations with hypocrites and the People of the Book.",
  5: "Among the last chapters revealed, named for the heavenly table spread requested by the disciples of Isa. It completes the dietary laws, contracts, ablution, and prescribed punishments, and stresses fulfilling covenants with Allah.",
  6: "Revealed in Makkah, this chapter demolishes idolatry with proofs from creation — the very cattle the pagans held sacred are Allah's creation, not His partners. It affirms tawhid, prophethood, and the Last Day with powerful rational arguments.",
  7: "Named for the heights between Paradise and Hell where the undecided will wait. This Makkan chapter retells the stories of Nuh, Hud, Salih, Lut, Shu'ayb, and Musa against Pharaoh, warning Makkah's rejecters through the fate of earlier nations.",
  8: "Revealed after the Battle of Badr, it legislates the distribution of war booty and draws the moral of that first victory: success comes from faith, unity, and trust in Allah — not from numbers or arms.",
  9: "The only chapter without Bismillah at its start. Revealed around the Tabuk expedition, it severs treaties with treacherous idolaters, exposes the hypocrites of Madinah, and closes with Allah's acceptance of sincere repentance.",
  10: "A Makkan chapter named for Prophet Yunus, swallowed by the great fish and saved through his repentance — proof that no people are beyond mercy if they turn back. It defends the Quran against the charge of sorcery and affirms divine wisdom.",
  11: "Named for Prophet Hud and the people of 'Ad. This Makkan chapter strings together the stories of Nuh, Hud, Salih, Ibrahim, Lut, Shu'ayb, and Musa, commanding the Prophet to remain steadfast as those messengers did against denial and mockery.",
  12: "Described in its opening as the best of stories. Revealed in Makkah, it narrates the complete story of Prophet Yusuf — betrayal by his brothers, slavery, prison, and rise to power in Egypt — a lesson in patience, chastity, and trust in Allah's plan.",
  13: "A Madinan chapter named for the thunder that glorifies Allah. It points to the signs in nature — rain, rivers, lightning — as proofs of resurrection and divine power, contrasting those who answer their Lord with those who reject Him.",
  14: "Centered on Prophet Ibrahim's heartfelt supplication for Makkah and its people. This Makkan chapter contrasts gratitude with ingratitude for Allah's blessings and warns that every nation that denied its messenger faced ruin.",
  15: "Named for the rocky dwellings of Thamud. It carries Allah's promise: 'Indeed, We sent down the Reminder, and indeed We will preserve it' — the guarantee of the Quran's protection — alongside the stories of earlier peoples and Iblis's envy of Adam.",
  16: "Called the chapter of blessings for its enumeration of Allah's favors: rain, crops, livestock, and the bee inspired to make honey 'wherein is healing for people'. Gratitude for these blessings, and warning against ingratitude, is its core message.",
  17: "Opens with the miraculous Night Journey of the Prophet from Makkah to Jerusalem. This Makkan chapter addresses Bani Isra'il, lays down moral commandments, and presents the Quran as guidance and healing for the believers.",
  18: "Named for the Sleepers of the Cave. Recited every Friday by tradition, it weaves four stories — the Cave, the two gardens, Musa and Khidr, and Dhul-Qarnayn — warning against attachment to wealth and power and affirming the resurrection.",
  19: "Named for Maryam, mother of Isa. This Makkan chapter recounts the miraculous births of Yahya and Isa, defends Maryam's honor, and presents Isa as a servant and prophet of Allah — not divine — alongside glimpses of Ibrahim, Musa, and other prophets.",
  20: "Opens with the mysterious letters Ta-Ha. Its heart is the detailed story of Musa: his call at the burning bush, the confrontation with Pharaoh's magicians, and the Exodus — consolation for the Prophet facing Makkan opposition.",
  21: "A procession of prophets — Ibrahim, Lut, Nuh, Dawud, Sulayman, Ayyub, Yunus, Zakariya, and Maryam — each rescued by Allah when they called on Him. Its refrain: all messengers brought the same message of one God.",
  22: "The chapter of Hajj: it commands the pilgrimage to the Ancient House, details its rites, permits fighting in self-defense for the wronged, and describes the awe of Resurrection Day when every soul will see its deeds.",
  23: "Opens with the portrait of the successful believers — humble in prayer, avoiding vain talk, giving charity, guarding chastity — then traces human creation from clay to resurrection as proof of Allah's power to raise the dead.",
  24: "Contains the luminous Verse of Light. Revealed in Madinah, it legislates modesty and the veil, the punishment for slander — revealed after the Ifk incident against Aisha — and the etiquette of marriage and entering homes.",
  25: "The Quran as the Criterion between truth and falsehood. This Makkan chapter answers the pagans' objections to the Prophet's humanity and the Quran's revelation, and describes the true servants of the Most Merciful.",
  26: "Named for its closing verses distinguishing guided poets from misguided ones. It retells the confrontations of Musa with Pharaoh, Ibrahim with his people, and of Nuh, Hud, Salih, Lut, and Shu'ayb with theirs.",
  27: "Named for the ant whose speech made Sulayman smile. It presents Sulayman's kingdom, the Queen of Sheba, and the stories of Musa and Salih — signs of Allah's dominion over all creatures, great and small.",
  28: "The most detailed Quranic account of Musa: his infancy in Pharaoh's palace, his exile, his call, and the drowning of Pharaoh. Revealed in Makkah to strengthen the Prophet with the precedent of Musa's long struggle.",
  29: "'Do people think they will be left to say we believe without being tested?' This Makkan chapter consoles the persecuted believers: the spider's web — frailest of houses — is the parable of false protectors besides Allah.",
  30: "Opens with a bold prophecy: the Romans, freshly defeated by the Persians, would triumph within a few years — and they did. A Makkan proof of the Quran's divine origin, urging reflection on the alternation of victory and defeat.",
  31: "Named for the wise Luqman and his timeless advice to his son: worship Allah alone, be grateful to parents, establish prayer, enjoin good, forbid evil, and bear trials with patience. A Makkan chapter on wisdom and gratitude.",
  32: "Named for the verse of prostration describing true believers who fall in prostration. It traces creation from clay, affirms the resurrection, and contrasts the believers' reward with the deniers' regret.",
  33: "Revealed around the Battle of the Trench, when confederate tribes besieged Madinah. It legislates the sanctity of the Prophet's household, abolishes adoptive sonship as blood kinship, and honors the Prophet's wives as mothers of the believers.",
  34: "Named for the people of Sheba, whose dam burst and destroyed their gardens — a parable of ingratitude. It also recalls Dawud and Sulayman, whose kingdoms showed what gratitude to Allah brings.",
  35: "The Originator of the heavens and the earth. This Makkan chapter marvels at creation — winds, seas, night and day — warns that Satan is an open enemy, and declares that none can withhold Allah's mercy once He grants it.",
  36: "Called the heart of the Quran in a well-known tradition. Opening with the letters Ya-Sin, it affirms the Prophet's mission, the resurrection, and Allah's absolute power, with the parable of the town that rejected its messengers.",
  37: "Opens with angels ranged in ranks. It recounts Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son, the rescue of the believers, Yunus's mission to Nineveh — ending with the declaration that Allah's party shall prevail.",
  38: "Named for the letter Sad. It presents Dawud's repentance, Sulayman's kingdom and trial, Ayyub's patience, and the story of Adam and Iblis — lessons for the Makkans who rejected their Prophet.",
  39: "The Groups driven to Paradise and to Hell. This Makkan chapter opens the doors of hope — 'do not despair of the mercy of Allah' — and describes the multitudes entering their eternal abodes in companies.",
  40: "Also called Al-Mu'min, for the believing man of Pharaoh's family who concealed his faith and pleaded for Musa. It proclaims Allah the Forgiver of sin and Acceptor of repentance, warning Pharaoh's people of the coming punishment.",
  41: "A Book whose verses are explained in detail. This Makkan chapter defends the Quran's clarity in Arabic, recounts the fates of 'Ad and Thamud, and promises that Allah will show His signs on the horizons and within themselves.",
  42: "Named for the verse praising those who conduct their affairs by mutual consultation. It unites all revelations as one religion, warns of the approaching Hour, and affirms that Allah answers those who believe and do good.",
  43: "The gilded ornaments of worldly life. This Makkan chapter exposes the emptiness of wealth and status — Pharaoh had gold, yet perished — and defends the Prophet against the mockery of the Makkan chiefs.",
  44: "Named for the smoke that will cover the sky before the Hour. It recalls the blessed night on which the Quran descended, Pharaoh's drowning, and the contrasting ends of the righteous and the wicked.",
  45: "The nations crouching on their knees on Judgment Day. This Makkan chapter warns those who mock Allah's signs and promises that every community will be called to its record and recompensed.",
  46: "Named for the wind-curved sandhills where 'Ad lived. It tells of Hud's warning to them, and of a company of jinn who heard the Quran recited and returned to their people as warners.",
  47: "Also called Al-Qital (Fighting). A Madinan chapter on struggle in Allah's cause: the fates of believers and disbelievers in war, the testing of hearts, and the call to spend, obey, and not turn back.",
  48: "Revealed after the Treaty of Hudaybiyyah — 'Indeed, We have granted you a clear victory.' It praises those who pledged beneath the tree, describes the Prophet's companions, and foretells the conquest of Makkah.",
  49: "The chapter of manners: lower your voices before the Prophet, verify news from unreliable sources, avoid mockery, suspicion, spying, and backbiting — 'the believers are but brothers, so make peace between your brothers.'",
  50: "Opens with the letter Qaf and an oath by the glorious Quran. A Makkan argument for resurrection: Allah, who created the heavens and knows every whisper of the soul, can surely raise the dead.",
  51: "Opens with oaths by the winds that scatter. It recounts Ibrahim's honored guests, the destruction of denying peoples, and commands remembrance — 'and remind, for indeed the reminder benefits the believers.'",
  52: "An oath by Mount Sinai and the Frequented House. This Makkan chapter warns of the Day when the sky sways with commotion, and affirms the Prophet is no soothsayer or madman, but a warner.",
  53: "By the star when it descends. The first chapter proclaimed publicly in Makkah with a prostration — even the pagans prostrated on hearing it. It defends the Prophet's ascension and the revelation he received.",
  54: "The Hour has drawn near and the moon has split — the Makkans witnessed the splitting yet called it magic. Its refrain after each destroyed nation: 'And We have made the Quran easy for remembrance.'",
  55: "The symphony of divine favors: 'Which of your Lord's favors will you deny?' — repeated thirty-one times across the blessings of creation, the two seas, and the Gardens promised to the God-fearing.",
  56: "The Inevitable Event — the Resurrection — dividing mankind into the foremost, the companions of the right, and the companions of the left, with vivid portraits of the destinies of each group.",
  57: "Named for iron, 'with mighty strength and benefits for people'. A Madinan call to spend in Allah's cause before death, contrasting the light of the believers with the darkness of the hypocrites on the Last Day.",
  58: "Allah heard the woman who pleaded about her husband — Khawlah, whose complaint abolished the pre-Islamic practice of zihar. It legislates charity before private consultation and condemns secret counsels of sin.",
  59: "On the exile of Banu Nadir. Its closing verses — among the most recited in the Quran — enumerate Allah's beautiful names: the King, the Holy, the Giver of Peace, the Almighty.",
  60: "Commands testing the faith of believing women who emigrate to Madinah, and forbids alliance with Allah's enemies — while permitting kindness and justice toward peaceful non-Muslims.",
  61: "Allah loves those who strive in His cause in solid ranks. It recalls Musa and Isa — Isa foretelling a messenger named Ahmad — and offers the trade that saves from painful punishment: belief and striving.",
  62: "Institutes the Friday congregational prayer: 'hasten to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade.' It rebukes those who were entrusted with the Torah yet carry it like donkeys loaded with books.",
  63: "Exposes the hypocrites of Madinah — their false oaths, their mockery, their boast to expel the honored from the city — and warns believers against letting wealth and children distract them from remembrance.",
  64: "The Day of Mutual Disillusion, when the deceived and the deceivers face one another. It calls to faith, obedience, and charity, and notes that wealth and children are but a trial.",
  65: "The chapter of divorce law: waiting periods, maintenance, and housing for divorced women — framed by the reminder that Allah provides for the God-fearing from where they do not expect.",
  66: "Revealed when the Prophet forbade himself something lawful to please his wives — 'why do you prohibit what Allah has made lawful for you?' It ends with the examples of believing women: Maryam and the wife of Pharaoh.",
  67: "Blessed is He in whose hand is dominion. A nightly recitation by prophetic tradition, it proclaims Allah's absolute sovereignty over creation and warns of the punishment awaiting the deniers.",
  68: "An oath by the pen and what they inscribe. It defends the Prophet — 'you are of a great moral character' — against charges of madness, and warns the Makkan chiefs with the parable of the garden owners.",
  69: "The Inevitable Reality. With scenes of Thamud, 'Ad, and Pharaoh destroyed for denial, it affirms the Judgment and the weighing of deeds — a Makkan thunderclap against those who reject the Hour.",
  70: "The stairways by which the angels ascend. A Makkan warning that the punishment the mockers demand will surely come, and a portrait of human impatience — cured only by prayer, charity, and certainty.",
  71: "The entire chapter is Nuh's mission across 950 years: his public and secret preaching, his people's deafness and arrogance, and his final supplication — a mirror for the Prophet's own perseverance in Makkah.",
  72: "A company of jinn overheard the Quran, believed, and returned to their people as warners. This Makkan chapter affirms the unseen world and declares that guidance reaches beyond mankind.",
  73: "Among the earliest revelations: 'O you enshrouded one, stand in prayer at night.' It commands night worship and slow, measured recitation as preparation for the weighty Word to come.",
  74: "'O you cloaked one, arise and warn!' — among the first commands of the mission. It orders purification, patience, and shunning idols, and warns of the Day the Trumpet is blown.",
  75: "An oath by the Day of Resurrection and the self-reproaching soul. The denier who asks 'when is the Day of Resurrection?' will see bones reassembled — Allah can shape even the fingertips.",
  76: "Also called Ad-Dahr (Time). A Madinan meditation on man's creation from a drop of fluid, the righteous who feed the poor, the orphan, and the captive for Allah's sake alone, and the Paradise awaiting them.",
  77: "An oath by the winds sent forth in succession. Its hammering refrain — 'Woe that Day to the deniers!' — repeats ten times through scenes of judgment and the fate of earlier peoples.",
  78: "About what do they question one another? — about the Great News. This Makkan chapter opens the final section of the Quran with the certainty of resurrection, the judgment, and the losers' regret.",
  79: "By the angels who draw souls gently or violently. It recounts Musa's mission to Pharaoh — who said 'I am your lord, most high' — and his drowning, a warning to the Makkan chiefs.",
  80: "He frowned and turned away when the blind man came. Revealed gently correcting the Prophet for preferring the chiefs over the sincere seeker Ibn Umm Maktum — a timeless lesson in valuing every believer.",
  81: "When the sun is folded up and the stars fall away. Cosmic upheaval on the Last Day, and the defense of the Quran as the word of a noble messenger — not the utterance of a madman or poet.",
  82: "When the sky is cleft asunder and the graves are overturned. A short Makkan chapter on man's ingratitude: 'What has deceived you concerning your Lord, the Generous?'",
  83: "Woe to those who cheat in measure — demanding full when they receive, giving short when they deal out. Their record is in Sijjin; the righteous are in 'Illiyyun, witnessing bliss.",
  84: "When the sky splits apart and the earth casts out its contents. Man labors toward his Lord and shall meet Him — receiving his book in his right hand, or behind his back.",
  85: "By the sky with its great constellations. It commemorates the People of the Ditch — believers burned alive by a tyrant king — consoling the persecuted Muslims of Makkah that tyrants never ultimately win.",
  86: "By the piercing night star. Every soul has a guardian over it; Allah, who created man from a gushing fluid, can resurrect him — and the Quran is a decisive word, not a jest.",
  87: "Glorify the name of your Lord, the Most High. A beloved chapter of the Prophet in the two Eids and Witr prayer, promising success to whoever purifies himself and remembers his Lord.",
  88: "'Has the news of the Overwhelming reached you?' Faces humbled and faces radiant on that Day — then the call to reflect on the camels, the sky, the mountains, and the spread-out earth.",
  89: "By the dawn and the ten nights. It recalls 'Ad, Thamud, and Pharaoh, and warns the restless soul — finding peace only in returning to its Lord, well-pleased and pleasing.",
  90: "An oath by this city — Makkah — while the Prophet dwells in it. The steep uphill path: freeing slaves and feeding the hungry on a day of scarcity — the choice between gratitude and ingratitude.",
  91: "By the sun and its brightness. The soul is inspired with both its wickedness and its piety — 'prosperous is he who purifies it.' Thamud's hamstrung she-camel seals the warning.",
  92: "By the night when it covers. Two paths diverge: the generous, God-fearing giver is eased toward comfort, while the miser who denies the good is eased toward hardship.",
  93: "Revealed to console the Prophet after a pause in revelation: 'Your Lord has not forsaken you, nor does He hate you.' Morning light after the night — a promise of divine care for every believer in hardship.",
  94: "Also called Alam Nashrah. 'Did We not expand your breast for you?' — with hardship comes ease, twice affirmed. A Makkan promise that consoles every strained heart.",
  95: "By the fig and the olive, Mount Sinai, and this secure city. Man is created in the finest form — then reduced to the lowest, except those who believe and do righteous deeds.",
  96: "The first revelation: 'Read! In the name of your Lord who created — created man from a clinging clot.' It rebukes the tyrant who forbids prayer and ends with the command: prostrate and draw near.",
  97: "The Night of Decree is better than a thousand months — the night on which the Quran descended, when the angels and the Spirit descend with every decree, peace until the break of dawn.",
  98: "A Madinan chapter: the People of the Book and the idolaters would not desist until the Clear Proof came — a Messenger reciting purified scriptures. True religion: sincere worship, prayer, and charity.",
  99: "When the earth is shaken with its mighty quaking and casts out its burdens. 'Whoever does an atom's weight of good shall see it' — a Madinan warning of perfect, inescapable reckoning.",
  100: "By the panting chargers striking sparks of fire. Man is ungrateful to his Lord and fierce in love of wealth — but the contents of breasts shall be laid bare on that Day.",
  101: "The Striking Calamity! People like scattered moths, mountains like carded wool. Heavy scales mean a pleasant life; light scales, the crushing abyss.",
  102: "Rivalry in worldly increase diverts you until you visit the graves. You shall surely see Hell with the eye of certainty — then be questioned about every blessing.",
  103: "By time, man is in loss — except those who believe, do righteous deeds, counsel truth, and counsel patience. Imam Shafi'i said this short chapter alone would suffice mankind.",
  104: "Woe to every slanderer and backbiter who hoards wealth, thinking it grants him eternity. His end: the crushing Fire that rises over the hearts.",
  105: "How Allah dealt with the companions of the elephant — Abraha's army marching on the Kaaba in the Prophet's birth year, destroyed by flocks of birds raining stones of baked clay.",
  106: "For the accustomed security of Quraysh — their winter and summer caravans. So let them worship the Lord of this House, who fed them against hunger and secured them against fear.",
  107: "Those who deny the Recompense: they repel the orphan, urge not the feeding of the poor, are heedless in their prayer, show off, and withhold even the smallest kindness.",
  108: "The shortest chapter: 'Indeed, We have granted you al-Kawthar.' So pray to your Lord and sacrifice — it is your hater who is cut off, not you.",
  109: "The chapter of disavowal: 'O disbelievers, I do not worship what you worship... to you your religion, and to me mine.' The Prophet recited it regularly in the Sunnah prayers of Fajr and Maghrib.",
  110: "The last complete chapter revealed. When Allah's help comes and people enter Islam in crowds — glorify your Lord with praise and seek His forgiveness. Revealed near the Farewell Pilgrimage.",
  111: "Also called Al-Lahab. The condemnation of Abu Lahab and his wife, the carrier of firewood — with a rope of palm fiber around her neck. Revealed in Makkah amid fierce persecution.",
  112: "The chapter of pure monotheism: 'Say: He is Allah, the One.' The Prophet said it equals one-third of the Quran — four verses defining Allah beyond all likeness or need.",
  113: "With An-Nas, one of the two chapters of refuge. Seek refuge with the Lord of the daybreak from the darkness of night, the blowers on knots, and the envy of the envier.",
  114: "The closing chapter of the Quran: refuge with the Lord, King, and God of mankind from the retreating whisperer — who whispers into the breasts of people, among jinn and men.",
};

